### AETHERIA — Complete Game Formula & Mathematics Bible

I would make this a dedicated technical upgrade: Aetheria Formula System V1.

The goal is to establish the mathematical rules behind every major gameplay system, so combat, exploration, crafting, creatures, NPCs, world simulation, economy, progression, terrain, and the 120-floor Great Labyrinth all follow consistent formulas.

This should be a central game-design and engineering specification, not a collection of random equations.

One important rule: formulas below are proposed starting models, not claims about formulas already implemented in your repository. We should preserve existing canon, inspect the actual scripts and data, and replace any existing formula only when its behaviour has been understood.

### 1\. Formula architecture

Every formula should have:

* A unique formula ID.

* A system and subsystem.

* Defined input variables.

* Units and valid ranges.

* A mathematical expression.

* A description of the result.

* Minimum and maximum constraints.

* Configuration parameters.

* Edge-case handling.

* Automated tests.

* Version information.

### Proposed formula registry

| Category         | Formula IDs | What they govern                       |
| ---------------- | ----------- | -------------------------------------- |
| Core mathematics | CORE        | Scaling, interpolation, probability    |
| Player           | PLY         | Attributes, levels, health, stamina    |
| Combat           | CMB         | Damage, defence, critical hits, status |
| Magic            | MAG         | Mana, spells, elements, cooldowns      |
| Enemies          | ENM         | AI threat, detection, combat decisions |
| Bosses           | BOS         | Phases, difficulty, encounter scaling  |
| Equipment        | EQP         | Weapons, armour, durability            |
| Inventory        | INV         | Weight, capacity, item stacking        |
| Crafting         | CRF         | Recipes, quality, success, materials   |
| Professions      | PRF         | Experience, proficiency, mastery       |
| World            | WRD         | Geography, distances, terrain          |
| Exploration      | EXP         | Discovery, surveying, rewards          |
| Movement         | MOV         | Walking, running, mounts, jumping      |
| Creatures        | CRT         | Growth, taming, bonding, needs         |
| Companions       | CMP         | Loyalty, commands, combat support      |
| NPCs             | NPC         | Schedules, memory, relationships       |
| Quests           | QST         | Progression, rewards, objectives       |
| Economy          | ECO         | Prices, supply, demand, trade          |
| Survival         | SUR         | Hunger, thirst, temperature            |
| Weather          | WTH         | Weather probability and intensity      |
| Ecology          | ECOLOG      | Wildlife populations and resources     |
| Dungeons         | DNG         | Encounters, rewards, room generation   |
| Labyrinth        | LAB         | 120-floor progression and difficulty   |
| Loot             | LOT         | Drop probabilities and rarity          |
| Progression      | PRG         | XP, ranks, unlocks                     |
| Save systems     | SAV         | Persistent state and data integrity    |
| Performance      | SYS         | Streaming budgets and simulation rates |

### 2\. Core mathematical functions

These should be implemented once and reused throughout the game. Avoid writing different versions of the same calculation in individual scripts.

### 2.1 Clamp

Restricts a value to an allowed range.

\\operatorname{Clamp}(x,a,b)=\\min(\\max(x,a),b)

Example: health cannot exceed maximum health or fall below zero.

### 2.2 Linear interpolation

\\operatorname{Lerp}(a,b,t)=a+(b-a)t

Used for:

* Weather transitions.

* Movement acceleration.

* Animation blending.

* Damage scaling.

* Environmental transitions.

* Lighting changes.

### 2.3 Normalized progression

P=\\operatorname{Clamp}\\left(\\frac{x-x_{\\min}}{x_{\\max}-x_{\\min}},0,1\\right)

Used to convert different measurements into a common 0–1 progression value.

### 2.4 Diminishing returns

R(x)=\\frac{x}{x+k}

Where controls how quickly the benefit decreases.

Useful for:

* Resistance.

* Detection.

* Crafting bonuses.

* Creature bonding.

* Relationship values.

### 2.5 Weighted random selection

P(i)=\\frac{w_i}{\\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_j}

Where is the weight of a possible outcome.

Use this for loot, encounters, weather selection, and procedural content selection.

### 2.6 Exponential smoothing

x_{t+\\Delta t}=x_t+(x_{\\text{target}}-x_t)(1-e^{-k\\Delta t})

Useful for smooth weather changes, environmental parameters, and simulation values.

Engineering requirement: All time-dependent formulas should use elapsed time, not assume a fixed frame rate.

### 3\. Player attributes, levels & progression

### 3.1 Player level experience

A configurable XP curve should govern character progression.

One possible model:

XP_{\\text{next}}(L)=A L^p+B L+C

Where:

* = current level.

* = progression tuning parameters.

* = curve steepness.

This provides a controllable progression curve without making every level cost the same amount.

Total XP required to reach level :

XP_{\\text{total}}(L)=\\sum_{i=1}^{L-1}XP_{\\text{next}}(i)

### 3.2 Attribute growth

S(L)=S_0+gL+b(L)

Where:

* = starting attribute.

* = attribute growth per level.

* = bonuses from equipment, abilities, quests, and other sources.

### 3.3 Health

HP_{\\max}=HP_0+a_V V+a_L L+HP_{\\text{bonus}}

Where is the relevant vitality attribute.

### 3.4 Stamina

STA_{\\max}=STA_0+s_E E+STA_{\\text{bonus}}

Where is the relevant endurance attribute.

### 3.5 Mana

MP_{\\max}=MP_0+m_I I+m_W W+MP_{\\text{bonus}}

Use the actual attributes defined by Aetheria's character system. The symbols and are illustrative and should not create new canonical attributes automatically.

### 3.6 Attribute-derived damage

D_{\\text{attribute}}=D_{\\text{base}}(1+\\alpha A)

Where is the relevant offensive attribute and is its scaling coefficient.

### 3.7 Attribute-derived resistance

R_{\\text{attribute}}=\\operatorname{Clamp}(rA,0,R_{\\max})

The resistance cap must be tuned to prevent characters from becoming completely immune to every threat.

