Combat should have a central damage pipeline. Weapon scripts, spell scripts, enemies, and bosses should not each calculate damage differently.

### 4.1 Physical damage

D_{\\text{raw}}=
(W+B)(1+S\\alpha)M

Where:

* = weapon damage.

* = ability bonus.

* = relevant player stat.

* = scaling coefficient.

* = attack multiplier.

### 4.2 Armour mitigation

A diminishing-return defence model:

D_{\\text{after armour}}
=
D_{\\text{raw}}
\\left(1-\\frac{A}{A+K_A}\\right)

Where is effective armour and controls the strength of armour scaling.

This avoids making each additional armour point equally powerful.

### 4.3 Resistance

D_{\\text{after resistance}}
=
D_{\\text{previous}}(1-R)

Where is resistance expressed as a decimal.

For example, 25% resistance means .

### 4.4 Critical hits

D_{\\text{critical}}=D_{\\text{normal}}C

Where is the critical damage multiplier.

Critical chance:

P_{\\text{crit}}=
\\operatorname{Clamp}(P_0+\\beta A,0,P_{\\max})

Use the relevant critical attribute and ensure equipment bonuses cannot push critical chance beyond its defined cap.

### 4.5 Hit probability

P_{\\text{hit}}=
\\operatorname{Clamp}
\\left(
P_0+\\alpha Acc-\\beta Eva,
P_{\\min},P_{\\max}
\\right)

Where:

* = attacker accuracy.

* = target evasion.

* = balance coefficients.

### 4.6 Damage over time

For a fixed damage-over-time effect:

D_{\\text{total}}=D_{\\text{tick}}N

Where is the number of valid ticks.

A more flexible implementation uses elapsed time:

D(t)=D_{\\text{rate}}\\Delta t

The effect should track its remaining duration, tick interval, stacking rules, and source.

### 4.7 Healing

H=(H_0+\\alpha M)(1+B_H)

Where is the relevant healing attribute and is the total healing bonus.

Final health:

HP_{\\text{new}}=\\min(HP_{\\max},HP_{\\text{current}}+H)

### 4.8 Stagger

Stagger=
\\frac{Impact}{Stability+K_S}

Apply stagger only when the result passes the target's threshold.

### 4.9 Blocking

D_{\\text{blocked}}=
D_{\\text{incoming}}(1-B)

Where is the block effectiveness.

Blocking may also consume stamina:

STA_{\\text{cost}}=C_0+D_{\\text{incoming}}C_1

### 4.10 Dodging

A dodge should use a combination of:

* Direction.

* Movement speed.

* Dodge duration.

* Invulnerability window, if the combat design supports one.

* Stamina cost.

* Recovery time.

The exact invulnerability window should be an animation and combat-design parameter, not a universal formula derived from frame rate.

### 4.11 Weapon durability

Durability_{\\text{new}}=
\\max(0,Durability_{\\text{current}}-C)

Where depends on attack type, material, and equipment quality.

Do not let durability loss become an invisible random punishment.

### 5\. Magic, elemental effects & abilities

### 5.1 Spell damage

D_{\\text{spell}}=
(B_s+\\alpha M)(1+E)(1+Q)

Where:

* = spell base damage.

* = relevant magic attribute.

* = elemental bonus.

* = spell-specific bonus.

### 5.2 Mana cost

Cost_{\\text{mana}}=
C_b(1-R_c)

Where is cost reduction, clamped to its allowed range.

### 5.3 Cooldown reduction

T_{\\text{cooldown}}=
T_0(1-R_{cd})

Set a minimum cooldown to prevent zero-time ability loops.

### 5.4 Elemental effectiveness

Use an authored interaction matrix rather than arbitrary elemental multipliers scattered through scripts.

| Interaction        | Multiplier example |
| ------------------ | ------------------ |
| Strong advantage   | 1.5                |
| Neutral            | 1.0                |
| Partial resistance | 0.75               |
| Strong resistance  | 0.5                |

These are sample tuning values. Aetheria's actual elemental relationships must come from its established magic canon.

### 5.5 Status buildup

B_{\\text{new}}=
B_{\\text{current}}+I_{\\text{effect}}R_{\\text{target}}

When buildup reaches the configured threshold, trigger the status effect and apply its stacking or reset rules.

### 5.6 Ability scaling

D_{\\text{ability}}=D_{\\text{weapon}}M_{\\text{ability}}+B_{\\text{ability}}

This keeps weapon attacks and abilities compatible with the same damage pipeline.

### 6\. Enemy AI & boss difficulty

### 6.1 Enemy detection

P_{\\text{detect}}=
F(d,v,l,s)

A detection model should consider:

* : distance.

* : player visibility.

* : lighting.

* : sound produced.

For a basic distance component:

D_{\\text{awareness}}=
\\operatorname{Clamp}(1-d/R,0,1)

Where is detection range.

### 6.2 Enemy threat

Threat=
w_D Damage+w_P Proximity+w_H Healing+w_T Time

Use threat to choose targets or priorities where applicable, without making enemies omniscient.

### 6.3 Enemy difficulty scaling

D_{\\text{enemy}}=
D_{\\text{base}}(1+\\alpha L_{\\text{enemy}})

Do not automatically scale every enemy to the player's level. Fixed regional difficulty and meaningful progression are important to the world.

### 6.4 Boss phases

Boss phase transitions should use explicit thresholds:

P_{\\text{phase}}=\\frac{HP_{\\text{current}}}{HP_{\\max}}

Example phase triggers:

* Phase 1: above 70%.

* Phase 2: 70% to 35%.

* Phase 3: below 35%.

These are proposed defaults only. Boss-specific mechanics may use timers, destroyed objects, arena states, or scripted events instead.

### 6.5 Boss encounter budget

B_{\\text{encounter}}=
B_{\\text{base}}+B_{\\text{mechanics}}+B_{\\text{arena}}

Use this to assess encounter complexity, not to automatically increase boss health until encounters become tedious.

### 7\. Equipment, inventory & item calculations

### 7.1 Equipment stat totals

S_{\\text{final}}=
S_{\\text{base}}+
\\sum S_{\\text{equipment}}+
\\sum S_{\\text{passive}}

### 7.2 Item quality

Q_{\\text{item}}=
Q_{\\text{material}}+
Q_{\\text{crafting}}+
Q_{\\text{modifier}}

Clamp quality to the permitted quality range.

### 7.3 Equipment scaling

Stat_{\\text{item}}=
Stat_{\\text{base}}(1+\\alpha Q)

Use a bounded quality coefficient to prevent excessive stat inflation.

### 7.4 Inventory weight

W_{\\text{inventory}}=
\\sum_{i=1}^{n}q_iw_i

Where is item quantity and is individual item weight.

### 7.5 Carry capacity

W_{\\max}=W_0+\\alpha STR

### 7.6 Movement penalty

P_{\\text{weight}}=
\\operatorname{Clamp}
\\left(\\frac{W_{\\text{inventory}}}{W_{\\max}},0,1\\right)

Use a tunable penalty curve to affect movement, stamina, or other systems. Do not automatically punish the player for reaching a particular inventory percentage unless the game design calls for it.

### 8\. Crafting, gathering & professions

### 8.1 Crafting success probability

P_{\\text{success}}=
\\operatorname{Clamp}
(P_0+\\alpha Skill+\\beta Tool+\\gamma Station-\\delta Difficulty,
P_{\\min},P_{\\max})

### 8.2 Crafted quality

Q=
Q_{\\text{recipe}}+
Q_{\\text{materials}}+
Q_{\\text{skill}}+
Q_{\\text{tool}}

### 8.3 Crafting time

T_{\\text{craft}}=
\\frac{T_0}{1+\\alpha Skill+\\beta Tool}

Set a minimum time so that crafting cannot become instant accidentally.

### 8.4 Gathering yield

Y=
\\max(0,Y_0+\\alpha Skill+\\beta Tool)

Add resource-specific yield caps and possible bonus yields.

### 8.5 Profession experience

XP_{\\text{profession}}=
XP_0M_{\\text{difficulty}}M_{\\text{quality}}

Prevent repeated trivial actions from providing unlimited progression.

### 8.6 Resource regeneration

R_{t+\\Delta t}=
\\min(R_{\\max},R_t+r\\Delta t)

For ecological resources, regeneration should depend on the resource's environment and depletion state.

