These formulas are particularly important to V10.

### 9.1 World distance

For two Unity positions:

d=\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2+(z_2-z_1)^2}

Use the project's actual coordinate scale to convert this distance into metres or kilometres.

### 9.2 Horizontal map distance

d_h=\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(z_2-z_1)^2}

This is useful for map display and horizontal navigation.

### 9.3 Route distance

D_{\\text{route}}=\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}d(P_i,P_{i+1})

Calculate using actual route segments, not a straight line between destination markers.

### 9.4 Travel time

T_{\\text{travel}}=
\\sum_i\\frac{d_i}{v_i}

Movement speed should account for:

* Terrain.

* Slope.

* Movement mode.

* Mount type.

* Obstacles.

* Status effects.

* Stamina.

* Road bonuses.

### 9.5 Terrain slope

Slope=\\arctan\\left(\\sqrt{
\\left(\\frac{\\partial h}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+
\\left(\\frac{\\partial h}{\\partial z}\\right)^2
}\\right)

This can influence walking speed, terrain material selection, vegetation, and traversal restrictions.

### 9.6 Elevation difference

\\Delta h=h(P_2)-h(P_1)

Use elevation difference for climb calculations and map information.

### 9.7 Fog of war

A simple exploration visibility model:

Visible(P)=
\\begin{cases}
1 & d(P,P_{\\text{player}})\\le R\\\\
0 & \\text{otherwise}
\\end{cases}

For realistic terrain visibility, include line-of-sight checks so mountains and obstacles can block discovery.

### 9.8 Region progress

Progress_{\\text{region}}=
\\frac{\\sum w_i Discovered_i}{\\sum w_i}

Where important discoveries can have different weights. Do not require finding every tiny collectible for a region to count as substantially explored.

### 9.9 Terrain generation

A basic multi-scale height function:

H(x,z)=
\\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}a_iN(f_ix,f_iz)

Where:

* = noise function.

* = amplitude.

* = frequency.

This is only one component of terrain generation. Aetheria should combine authored terrain, large-scale landforms, drainage, erosion, and controlled procedural detail.

### 9.10 River flow

A simplified drainage accumulation model:

Flow(P)=Rainfall(P)+\\sum Flow(P_{\\text{upstream}})

Use terrain flow direction to identify downstream cells. River widths can then be derived from accumulated flow, subject to minimum and maximum widths.

### 9.11 World map coordinate conversion

P_{\\text{world}}=T(P_{\\text{map}})

The conversion must be calibrated against the current repository's map coordinates. Do not assume that the existing `at` and `radius` values are metres.

