### 10.1 Creature growth

Growth=\\operatorname{Clamp}\\left(\\frac{Age}{MaturityAge},0,1\\right)

Creature stats can scale with growth:

Stat_{\\text{creature}}=
Stat_{\\text{juvenile}}+
Growth(Stat_{\\text{adult}}-Stat_{\\text{juvenile}})

### 10.2 Bonding

Bond_{\\text{new}}=
\\operatorname{Clamp}
(Bond_{\\text{current}}+\\Delta B,0,100)

Bonding gain can depend on:

* Feeding.

* Training.

* Successful commands.

* Exploration together.

* Combat cooperation.

* Care and attention.

Repeated identical actions should have diminishing rewards.

### 10.3 Creature trust

Trust_{\\text{new}}=
Trust_{\\text{current}}+
Care+Training-Suffering

Use separate trust and bonding values if they represent different gameplay concepts.

### 10.4 Mount stamina

STA_{\\text{mount,new}}=
\\operatorname{Clamp}
(STA_{\\text{mount}}-C_{\\text{movement}}\\Delta t+
R_{\\text{recovery}}\\Delta t,0,STA_{\\max})

### 10.5 Companion effectiveness

Effectiveness=
Base+
\\alpha Bond+
\\beta Training+
\\gamma Equipment

Apply appropriate caps to prevent companion stats from scaling without limit.

### 10.6 Creature population

A simplified population model:

\\frac{dN}{dt}=rN\\left(1-\\frac{N}{K}\\right)-H

Where:

* = population.

* = reproduction rate.

* = environmental carrying capacity.

* = losses from hunting, predators, and other causes.

This is useful for the ecological simulation, but it should not run at full detail for every creature in every unloaded region.

### 11\. NPCs, relationships & world simulation

### 11.1 Relationship progression

Relationship_{\\text{new}}=
\\operatorname{Clamp}
(Relationship_{\\text{current}}+\\sum Interaction_i, -100,100)

Interactions can be weighted by their importance and context.

### 11.2 NPC memory importance

MemoryScore=
Impact\\times Recency\\times Relevance

One possible recency function:

Recency=e^{-\\lambda t}

Where is elapsed time.

Important memories should not simply disappear because time passes. Major story events should be preserved separately from temporary memory.

### 11.3 NPC daily schedule

Activity(t)=Schedule(NPC,t,Location,WorldState)

Use authored schedules and condition-based overrides rather than randomizing every NPC's location every frame.

### 11.4 NPC needs

Need_{\\text{new}}=
\\operatorname{Clamp}(Need_{\\text{current}}+Rate\\Delta t,0,100)

Needs can influence NPC activities, but should not make every NPC perform a complex simulation continuously.

### 11.5 World event probability

P_{\\text{event}}=
\\operatorname{Clamp}(P_0M_{\\text{region}}M_{\\text{world state}},0,1)

Use this for appropriate ambient events, not to randomly override major authored story events.

### 12\. Quests, rewards & exploration discoveries

### 12.1 Quest difficulty

Difficulty=
w_L Level+
w_R RegionRank+
w_C CombatChallenge+
w_O ObjectiveComplexity

### 12.2 Quest reward

Reward=
BaseReward\\times DifficultyMultiplier\\times QualityMultiplier

Quest rewards should also account for fixed narrative rewards, unique items, and progression gates.

### 12.3 Quest progress

Progress=
\\frac{\\sum CompletedObjectives_i}{\\sum RequiredObjectives_i}

Some objectives may be optional or branching. Represent those explicitly rather than forcing every quest into a simple percentage.

### 12.4 Exploration reward

Reward_{\\text{discovery}}=
BaseReward\\times Rarity\\times DiscoveryDifficulty

### 12.5 Discovery rarity

Use weighted selection:

P(i)=\\frac{w_i}{\\sum w_j}

Avoid generating the same category of discovery repeatedly in nearby locations. Add regional uniqueness and recent-discovery tracking.

### 13\. Economy, trading & prices

### 13.1 Item price

Price=
BasePrice\\times Quality\\times Rarity\\times Condition

### 13.2 Supply and demand

Price_{\\text{market}}=
Price_{\\text{base}}
\\left(\\frac{Demand}{Supply+\\epsilon}\\right)^\\alpha

Where prevents division by zero.

### 13.3 Merchant buying price

Price_{\\text{buy}}=
Price_{\\text{market}}(1+Markup)

### 13.4 Merchant selling price

Price_{\\text{sell}}=
Price_{\\text{market}}(1-Discount)

### 13.5 Regional economy

Prices can vary by:

* Resource availability.

* Settlement size.

* Transport difficulty.

* Regional demand.

* Merchant specialization.

* Local events.

* Supply disruptions.

Do not allow market simulation to produce infinite prices or negative prices. Always define lower and upper bounds.

### 14\. Survival, cooking & environmental conditions

### 14.1 Hunger

H_{t+\\Delta t}=
\\operatorname{Clamp}(H_t-r_H\\Delta t,0,100)

### 14.2 Thirst

T_{t+\\Delta t}=
\\operatorname{Clamp}(T_t-r_T\\Delta t,0,100)

### 14.3 Temperature exposure

E_{\\text{temperature}}=
T_{\\text{environment}}-
T_{\\text{comfort}}

The actual exposure should account for clothing, weather, shelter, movement, and environmental hazards.

### 14.4 Cooking quality

Q_{\\text{food}}=
Q_{\\text{ingredients}}+
Q_{\\text{recipe}}+
Q_{\\text{cooking skill}}+
Q_{\\text{equipment}}

### 14.5 Food recovery

Recovery=
BaseRecovery\\times QualityMultiplier

All survival penalties should be clearly communicated and configurable. Avoid making survival systems unnecessarily punishing.

### 15\. Weather, seasons & ecology

### 15.1 Weather selection

P(W_i)=
\\frac{w_i(R,T,S,H)}{\\sum_j w_j(R,T,S,H)}

Where:

* = region.

* = temperature.

* = season.

* = humidity.

### 15.2 Temperature by elevation

A simplified atmospheric model:

T(h)=T_0-\\Gamma h

Where is a configurable temperature lapse rate.

### 15.3 Rain accumulation

Rain_{\\text{total}}=
\\sum RainRate_i\\Delta t

### 15.4 Seasonal vegetation

Growth_{\\text{vegetation}}=
f(Temperature,Water,Season,Soil)

### 15.5 Ecological resource regeneration

R_{\\text{new}}=
\\operatorname{Clamp}(R_{\\text{current}}+
Regeneration-Consumption,0,R_{\\max})

### 15.6 Weather transitions

Weather_t=
Weather_{t-1}+
(Weather_{\\text{target}}-Weather_{t-1})\\alpha

Use elapsed-time smoothing rather than abrupt random weather changes.

