### 16.1 Dungeon difficulty

D_{\\text{dungeon}}=
D_{\\text{base}}+
\\alpha RegionRank+
\\beta DungeonTier+
\\gamma EncounterComplexity

### 16.2 Encounter budget

B_{\\text{encounter}}=
B_{\\text{base}}\\times DifficultyMultiplier

Allocate that budget between enemies, hazards, environmental puzzles, and other encounter elements.

### 16.3 Loot rarity

P_{\\text{rarity}}=
\\frac{w_{\\text{rarity}}}{\\sum w_i}

Then select an item within the chosen rarity category.

### 16.4 Drop chance

P_{\\text{drop}}=
\\operatorname{Clamp}(P_0+B_{\\text{luck}},0,P_{\\max})

### 16.5 Labyrinth floor progression

For a preliminary mathematical model:

D(n)=D_0+an+bn^2

Where is the floor number.

However, a linear or quadratic formula should not dictate every floor. The 120 floors should retain their authored identities, unique mechanics, and progression pacing.

Use the formula to establish a baseline challenge curve, then apply individual floor overrides.

### 16.6 Floor reward scaling

Reward(n)=R_0(1+kn)^p

Set an upper limit and account for unique story rewards.

### 16.7 Boss difficulty by floor

BossPower(n)=B_0(1+\\alpha n+\\beta n^2)

Use this only as an initial balance curve. Unique bosses should be designed individually.

### 17\. Game systems, performance & technical formulas

Not every formula needs to affect gameplay. Some should govern how the game runs.

### 17.1 World streaming distance

d=\\sqrt{(x_p-x_s)^2+(z_p-z_s)^2}

Use distance to determine sector loading priority, with separate loading and unloading thresholds.

### 17.2 Simulation frequency

f_{\\text{simulation}}=g(D,Importance,Activity)

Nearby gameplay-critical objects receive full simulation. Distant objects can use reduced simulation or aggregated state.

### 17.3 Memory budget

M_{\\text{total}}=
M_{\\text{terrain}}+
M_{\\text{textures}}+
M_{\\text{meshes}}+
M_{\\text{objects}}+
M_{\\text{systems}}

Each subsystem should have a measured budget appropriate to target hardware.

### 17.4 Frame time

T_{\\text{frame}}=
T_{\\text{CPU}}+T_{\\text{GPU}}

This is a simplified conceptual model: CPU and GPU work can overlap, so actual frame timing must be measured with Unity profiling tools.

### 17.5 Loading priority

Priority=
w_d\\frac{1}{d+\\epsilon}
+w_v Visibility
+w_m MovementDirection
+w_q GameplayImportance

This can prioritize sectors that are close, visible, in the player's direction, or required for active gameplay.

### 18\. Formula implementation standards

This is where the mathematical design becomes maintainable code.

I recommend one central formula library with separate gameplay modules.

```text
Aetheria/
└── Systems/
    └── FormulaSystem/
        ├── Core/
        │   ├── FormulaRegistry
        │   ├── FormulaMath
        │   ├── FormulaConfig
        │   └── FormulaValidation
        │
        ├── Player/
        ├── Combat/
        ├── Magic/
        ├── Enemies/
        ├── Bosses/
        ├── Equipment/
        ├── Inventory/
        ├── Crafting/
        ├── Professions/
        ├── World/
        ├── Exploration/
        ├── Movement/
        ├── Creatures/
        ├── Companions/
        ├── NPCs/
        ├── Quests/
        ├── Economy/
        ├── Survival/
        ├── Weather/
        ├── Ecology/
        ├── Dungeons/
        ├── Labyrinth/
        ├── Loot/
        ├── Progression/
        └── Performance/
```

### Formula configuration

Store tuning values outside individual gameplay scripts.

Example:

```json
{
  "formulaId": "combat.physical_damage",
  "version": 1,
  "enabled": true,
  "parameters": {
    "attributeScaling": 0.025,
    "minimumDamage": 1.0,
    "maximumMultiplier": 5.0
  }
}
```

The values are illustrative. Final coefficients must come from balancing tests.

### Required formula testing

Every formula should have:

* Normal input tests.

* Minimum input tests.

* Maximum input tests.

* Negative input tests.

* Zero-value tests.

* Extreme-value tests.

* Repeated execution tests.

* Save/load compatibility tests where applicable.

* Performance tests where relevant.

* Regression tests against existing game behaviour.

### Important mathematical safeguards

* Avoid division by zero.

* Avoid NaN and infinity.

* Clamp values at explicitly defined boundaries.

* Use consistent units.

* Use floating-point calculations where appropriate.

* Use integer types for discrete quantities.

* Use deterministic random seeds where reproducibility matters.

* Avoid frame-rate-dependent calculations.

* Avoid unbounded exponential growth.

* Avoid uncontrolled stacking of bonuses.

* Document rounding rules.

* Separate simulation time from real-world time.

### 19\. Formula development roadmap

| Phase | Deliverable                              |
| ----- | ---------------------------------------- |
| 1     | Audit all existing gameplay calculations |
| 2     | Build central formula registry           |
| 3     | Implement shared mathematical functions  |
| 4     | Player progression and attributes        |
| 5     | Combat and magic                         |
| 6     | Equipment and inventory                  |
| 7     | Crafting and professions                 |
| 8     | Movement and world geography             |
| 9     | Creatures and companions                 |
| 10    | NPCs and quests                          |
| 11    | Economy and survival                     |
| 12    | Weather and ecology                      |
| 13    | Dungeons and Labyrinth                   |
| 14    | Loot and rewards                         |
| 15    | Save compatibility and validation        |
| 16    | Full balancing and regression testing    |

### 20\. Master directive for the development agent

# AETHERIA — COMPLETE FORMULA SYSTEM
## Mathematical Architecture, Gameplay Balancing & Simulation Framework

### PROJECT REQUIREMENTS

Develop a unified mathematical framework for Aetheria.

The game is Unity 6, URP, third-person, 3D and strictly single-player.

The objective is to establish consistent, configurable, documented and tested formulas for every major gameplay and technical system.

### ABSOLUTE REQUIREMENTS

1. Inspect the repository before implementation.
2. Identify existing formulas and calculations.
3. Document current behaviour before replacing anything.
4. Preserve established game canon and progression.
5. Do not invent attributes or mechanics that contradict existing design.
6. Do not duplicate existing systems unnecessarily.
7. Centralize reusable calculations.
8. Make balancing coefficients configurable.
9. Validate all inputs and outputs.
10. Add automated tests for every formula.

### REQUIRED FORMULA MODULES

Implement or audit:

- Core mathematical functions.
- Player statistics and progression.
- Experience and leveling.
- Health, stamina and mana.
- Physical combat.
- Magic and elemental damage.
- Critical hits and accuracy.
- Armour and resistance.
- Healing and status effects.
- Blocking, dodging and stagger.
- Enemy AI and threat.
- Boss difficulty and phases.
- Weapon and armour statistics.
- Equipment durability.
- Inventory and carrying capacity.
- Item quality and rarity.
- Crafting and gathering.
- Profession progression.
- Movement and traversal.
- Mount movement and stamina.
- Creature growth and bonding.
- Companion effectiveness.
- NPC relationships and memories.
- Quest progress and rewards.
- Economy and supply/demand.
- Survival and cooking.
- Weather and seasons.
- Ecological populations.
- World geography and distances.
- Terrain generation.
- Exploration and cartography.
- Dungeon difficulty.
- Loot generation.
- The 120-floor Great Labyrinth.
- World streaming.
- Performance and memory management.
- Save-state integrity.

### FORMULA REGISTRY

Every formula must have:

- Unique identifier.
- System name.
- Formula version.
- Input definitions.
- Input units.
- Output definition.
- Mathematical expression.
- Configurable coefficients.
- Valid input ranges.
- Output limits.
- Edge-case handling.
- Automated test coverage.

### CENTRALIZED IMPLEMENTATION

Create a central formula architecture with modular subsystems.

Do not scatter mathematical constants throughout unrelated gameplay scripts.

All shared calculations should use central reusable methods.

Use configuration assets or data files for tunable parameters.

Avoid hardcoding balance values into multiple locations.

### BALANCE REQUIREMENTS

Create test scenarios for:

- Low-level characters.
- Mid-progression characters.
- High-level characters.
- Different equipment combinations.
- Different creature and companion progression levels.
- Different enemy ranks.
- Boss encounters.
- Crafting progression.
- Regional economy variations.
- Exploration rewards.
- Labyrinth progression.

Check for runaway scaling, trivialized combat, impossible difficulty spikes, infinite reward loops and excessive resource generation.

### TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS

- Prevent division by zero.
- Prevent NaN and infinity.
- Define rounding behaviour.
- Define stacking behaviour.
- Define caps and minimum values.
- Use consistent units.
- Use elapsed time for time-based calculations.
- Use deterministic randomness where required.
- Preserve save compatibility.
- Provide formula configuration validation.
- Provide debug visibility for important calculations.

### DEVELOPER TOOLS

Create a Formula Debugger that allows developers to:

- Select a formula.
- Inspect its parameters.
- Enter test values.
- Calculate outputs.
- Compare different configurations.
- View minimum and maximum behaviour.
- Run test cases.
- Inspect formula versions.
- Identify invalid configurations.

Create a Balance Simulator for combat, progression, loot, crafting and Labyrinth difficulty.

### IMPLEMENTATION STRATEGY

Work in stages.

First audit the current project.

Then create the core formula library.

Next implement player, combat and progression calculations.

After that integrate equipment, crafting, world systems, creatures, NPCs, economy and simulation.

Finally perform balancing, regression tests, performance tests and save compatibility validation.

Do not rewrite all gameplay systems simultaneously.

### ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

The formula system is complete when:

- Every major gameplay system has documented calculations.
- Shared mathematical operations are centralized.
- Coefficients are configurable.
- Formulas have valid input and output ranges.
- Automated tests pass.
- Existing gameplay remains compatible.
- Combat and progression can be simulated.
- Formula changes can be tested without editing unrelated gameplay scripts.
- No critical mathematical or save-data errors remain.

### FINAL OBJECTIVE

Build a complete mathematical foundation for Aetheria.

Every major system should operate using consistent, understandable, testable and balanced rules.

The formulas should support the game's design, not force the game into arbitrary mathematical behaviour.

### My recommendation: build a balance simulator alongside the formulas

This is probably the most valuable additional tool.

### Example: Combat formula tester

Illustrative formula playground — not Aetheria's verified current balance.

Weapon base damage

### 40

Strength

### 30

Attribute scaling

### 0.025

Enemy armour

### 20

Apply critical damage (×1.5)

Raw damage

### 70.00

Final damage

### 58.33

Example calculation: raw damage = weapon × (1 + strength × scaling); armour mitigation uses armour / (armour + 100).

The balance simulator should eventually let you test entire character builds, weapons, enemies, bosses, creatures, crafting recipes, and progression curves before changing the actual game.

The key is to create one mathematical source of truth for Aetheria. That way, if you change a damage coefficient or XP curve, every system using it can update consistently, and you can test the impact before releasing the change.
